    "slug": "somali-upper-house-advances-bill-to-establish-meteorological-agency",
    "summary": "Somalia's Upper House passed the second reading of a bill to create a national meteorological agency, aiming to improve weather forecasting and climate data services.",
    "content": "Mogadishu, October 14, 2026 — Somalia's Upper House of the Federal Parliament has moved forward with a bill to establish a national meteorological agency. The legislation, which passed its second reading on Tuesday, is intended to strengthen weather forecasting and climate data services across the country.\n\nThe session, the ninth of the eighth parliamentary term, was chaired by Upper House Speaker Abdi Hashi Abdullahi, with Second Deputy Speaker Abdullahi Ali Hersi Timacadde also in attendance. Twenty-eight senators were present, meeting the quorum required for proceedings. The bill's presentation was led by the chair of the relevant committee, though further details on the committee's report were not immediately available.\n\nThe proposed agency would centralize meteorological services, which are currently fragmented among various government bodies and international partners. Somalia has long lacked a robust national framework for weather monitoring, leaving it vulnerable to droughts, floods, and other climate shocks. The new agency is expected to provide reliable forecasts and early warnings to support agriculture, disaster preparedness, and infrastructure planning.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the establishment of a national meteorological agency could have significant implications. Many diaspora members invest in businesses and properties in Somalia and rely on accurate weather information for agricultural ventures and remittance-backed projects. Improved climate data can also enhance disaster response, reducing the need for emergency diaspora fundraising during crises. Additionally, the agency may create opportunities for skilled diaspora professionals in meteorology and related fields to contribute to Somalia's development.\n\nThe bill now proceeds to further parliamentary stages before becoming law. If enacted, the agency would mark a step toward building Somalia's institutional capacity to address climate-related challenges. The international community has frequently emphasized the need for such infrastructure as part of broader state-building efforts.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/14/aqalka-sare-oo-ka-dooday-hindise-sharciyeedka-dhismaha-hayadda-saadaasha-hawada/",
