    "content": "The Speaker of the People's Assembly of the Federal Parliament of Somalia, Abdikadir Mohamed Nur (Jamac), held a meeting on Monday with the Assembly's Committee on Justice, Awqaf, and Religious Affairs. The primary focus of the discussion was the review and modernization of Somalia's Penal Code, according to a statement from the Speaker's office.\n\nThe meeting was also attended by the Minister of Justice and Constitutional Affairs, Hassan Moalim Mahmud. Participants examined the draft Penal Code bill currently before the People's Assembly, which aims to align the legislation with ongoing social, economic, and technological changes in the country.\n\nThe Penal Code reform has been under consideration for several months as part of broader efforts to update Somalia's legal framework. The proposed amendments seek to address gaps in the existing law, which dates back to the pre-civil war era, and to incorporate contemporary legal standards.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these legislative changes could have significant implications. Many diaspora members maintain close ties to Somalia, including property ownership, business investments, and family connections. An updated Penal Code may affect legal procedures related to these interests, as well as provide clearer guidelines for judicial cooperation between Somali authorities and international partners.\n\nThe committee's review is part of the parliamentary process, and further deliberations are expected before the bill is presented for a full vote. The Speaker's involvement underscores the priority given to legal reform by the current leadership.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/14/guddoomiyaha-golaha-shacabka-oo-kulan-la-qaatay-guddiga-garsoorka-iyo-arrimaha-diinta/",
